/lni/dragonboat/v3/internal/logdb\",\"Output\":\"PASS\\n\"}\n{\"Time\":\"2026-10-04T18:45:12.497196233+08:00\",\"Action\":\"output\",\"Package\":\"github.com/lni/dragonboat/v3/internal/logdb\",\"Output\":\"ok  \\tgithub.com/lni/dragonboat/v3/internal/logdb\\t0.073s\\n\"}\n{\"Time\":\"2026-10-04T18:45:12.49727755+08:00\",\"Action\":\"pass\",\"Package\":\"github.com/lni/dragonboat/v3/internal/logdb\",\"Elapsed\":0.074}\n{\n  \"action\": \"review\",\n  \"artifact_id\": \"b3582539d580412caa5467e8fc2f56c5\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Review the fixed first-version check against C-read-context version 1 and its actual trusted execution. A complete per-origin context mismatch is observed; scope remains Peer readiness identity, not client liveness or application linearizability.\",\n  \"review_items\": [\n    {\n      \"aspect\": \"checker_correspondence\",\n      \"status\": \"no_issue_found\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"S-context-contract\",\n        \"S-read-context-test\",\n        \"S-prefix-context-test\",\n        \"S-read-queue\",\n        \"S-read-response\",\n        \"S-follower-read\",\n        \"S-client-batches\",\n        \"S-peer-launch\",\n        \"S-bootstrap\",\n        \"S-peer-update\",\n        \"S-peer-export\",\n        \"S-peer-members\",\n        \"S-memory-db\",\n        \"S-logdb-interface\",\n        \"S-peer-admission\",\n        \"S-message-term\",\n        \"S-read-send\",\n        \"S-replication-recv\",\n        \"S-ticks\",\n        \"S-engine-order\",\n        \"S-node-effects\"\n      ],\n      \"rationale\": \"The fixed plan and harness match C-read-context v1: preservation of the SystemCtx identifying each successfully confirmed remote operation. S-context-contract explicitly defines SystemCtx as operation identity; S-read-context-test checks remote readiness against the initiating context; S-prefix-context-test preserves each returned earlier status context. These support the equality oracle independently of the suspect emitter. This is not an eventual-success assertion. The driver uses Launch and three distinct bootstrap addresses, processes real bootstrap entries and the elected no-op, persists each update into the existing TestLogDB before delivery, and acknowledges updates through Commit and NotifyRaftLastApplied. It sets no private raft fields and overrides no handler. Only memory persistence, synchronous application of bootstrap/no-op entries, clock scheduling, fixed opaque contexts and transport scheduling are substituted. No crash, later membership change or application command occurs. Election happens via Tick and actual votes, and the setup guard witnesses a leader with current-term committed entry plus common applied prefix. The recorded setup was leader 1, term 2, commit 4. Every delivered message is an actual Peer output, with Peer.Handle retaining membership and term checks. Earlier-context heartbeat replies are held while the later request is admitted; this preserves the investigated overlap. Afterward all held replies are delivered, independent of comparison outcome. Prefix-confirmation events observe live registered status pointers removed by the same-term heartbeat handler, retain each status context and index, and precede result events. There is no intervening role change or other pending-state reset in the read phase; removal is attributable to readIndex.confirm. The fixed monitor uses admission_alias=confirmed, with declared references to admitted context/term. It correlates by scenario and origin, independently of the compared context; exactly one request exists per origin and duplicate origins abort setup. Execution b5f3cb2c346040a9b22ca064ee24a691 admits origin 2 as 301:101 and origin 3 as 302:202. An actual reply from voter 2 for 302:202 confirms both statuses at index 4 in term 2. The response delivered to origin 2 and its GetUpdate ReadyToReads copy both contain 302:202; origin 3 also contains 302:202, correctly for its own request. Monitor witness 6 therefore differs from admitted event 1 after confirmed event 3; the independent origin 3 comparison uses events 2, 4 and 8. Parsing reports no errors and both comparisons are complete. Readiness is copied directly from the target Update before Commit clears it, so the mismatch is an exported protocol result, not a fabricated client cache value. The matching wire response corroborates the source branch that uses triggering m.Hint/m.HintHigh instead of each s.ctx. End diagnostics show two admitted operations, zero pending reads, zero queued and zero held messages. Process PASS establishes only completed harness execution; the property monitor separately reports violated. No concrete correspondence issue was found in this scope. Explained alternatives and contrary scope evidence: The latest remote request retains its correct context; local-origin statuses use s.ctx correctly in source. The defect is conditional on a confirmed prefix containing an earlier distinct remote context. The client API permits timeout outcomes and pendingReadIndex requires exact context; neither establishes a client deadline violation in this Peer-only execution. All compared read indexes are 4 with applied index 4; no stale value or index regression is demonstrated.\",\n      \"limitations\": [\n        \"Real transport, NodeHost pending-batch machinery and an application state machine are not executed; client timeout is a potential consequence inferred from exact-key consumption, not an observed endpoint.\",\n        \"TestLogDB is memory-backed and no crash occurs. Durability, recovery, configuration changes, context collisions and other schedules remain outside the claim.\",\n        \"Queue removal is treated as successful confirmation only in this fixed same-term read-only message phase, where source and actual messages exclude reset/removal alternatives.\",\n        \"No claim of infinite stalling, numeric deadline violation or whole-cluster linearizability failure.\"\n      ],\n      \"report_title\": \"\\u8fdc\\u7aef ReadIndex \\u6279\\u91cf\\u786e\\u8ba4\\u66ff\\u6362\\u4e86\\u8f83\\u65e9\\u8bf7\\u6c42\\u7684\\u6807\\u8bc6\",\n      \"report_answer\": \"\\u4e09\\u8282\\u70b9\\u3001\\u56fa\\u5b9a\\u4efb\\u671f\\u4e0e\\u6210\\u5458\\u914d\\u7f6e\\u4e0b\\uff0c\\u8282\\u70b9 2 \\u7684\\u8bf7\\u6c42\\u4ee5 301:101 \\u6ce8\\u518c\\u5e76\\u83b7\\u786e\\u8ba4\\uff0c\\u4f46\\u5176\\u6536\\u5230\\u7684\\u54cd\\u5e94\\u53ca ReadyToRead \\u5747\\u4e3a\\u8282\\u70b9 3 \\u7684 302:202\\u3002\\u68c0\\u67e5\\u9a8c\\u8bc1\\u7684\\u662f Peer \\u5c42\\u64cd\\u4f5c\\u6807\\u8bc6\\u4f20\\u64ad\\u9519\\u8bef\\uff1b\\u672a\\u6267\\u884c\\u5ba2\\u6237\\u7aef\\u8d85\\u65f6\\u6216\\u5e94\\u7528\\u5c42\\u9648\\u65e7\\u8bfb\\u3002\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"feedback\": {\n    \"ref_ids\": [\n      \"a118363ef443443ca4bcfe8f5be0f751\",\n      \"b3582539d580412caa5467e8fc2f56c5\",\n      \"b5f3cb2c346040a9b22ca064ee24a691\",\n      \"B-read-confirm\",\n      \"B-read-deliver\",\n      \"S-read-response\",\n      \"S-read-queue\",\n      \"S-context-contract\"\n    ],\n    \"answered\": \"Trusted execution observed the earlier remote request identity replaced by the later confirming context in both delivered response and originating Peer readiness, despite actual successful confirmation retaining the earlier status identity. The same-history latest request retained its own context. The fixed monitor comparison is complete and this review finds no concrete correspondence issue for C-read-context v1.\",\n    \"remaining\": [\n      \"Actual NodeHost client timeout and application-read consequences were not measured.\",\n      \"Other legal membership-transition and authority histories remain independent investigation questions.\"\n    ],\n    \"understanding\": \"updated\",\n    \"rationale\": \"This answers the selected per-operation identity propagation proposition. Source identifies remote response field selection as the cause; broader progress or safety claims are not licensed.\"\n  }\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
